   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 2270094,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 406522,
    "poster_id": 62562,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" > be a prime and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > a positive integer such that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/c/57c66ce656c0d66ab6ded929d357960b11105e04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\le p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" >.</span> We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96dd6ec1dc4ad7520fbdc78fcdbec9edd068d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" > is a polynomial in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc78abe277078318648d69381180248b72777be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb Z[x]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" > such that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/8/e58b46c4431936312fa198e6b957fb42a05bc733.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" > we have <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/9/609f4417a075800d186813a9d91b71e4d2b9babb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^k | f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"20\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<b>(a)</b> Prove that there exist polynomials <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/1/521bb978ba362f9c22485db0d33b1ba323783f84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0(x),\\ldots,A_k(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"18\" > all in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc78abe277078318648d69381180248b72777be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb Z[x]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" > such that<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/9/3a988c347d64f60a9006ad4c0a5db31810c2dc69.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[ f(x)=\\sum_{i=0}^{k} (x^p-x)^ip^{k-i}A_i(x),\\]\"  width=\"235\" height=\"51\" ><br>\n<br>\n<b>(b)</b> Find a counter example for each prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" > and each <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/b/d0b05e72cd8984d693b8f4db147a67684a5d8d1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &gt; p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Let $p$ be a prime and $k$ a positive integer such that $k \\le p$. We know that $f(x)$ is a polynomial in $\\mathbb Z[x]$ such that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ we have $p^k | f(x)$. \n\n[b](a)[/b] Prove that there exist polynomials $A_0(x),\\ldots,A_k(x)$ all in $\\mathbb Z[x]$ such that\n\\[ f(x)=\\sum_{i=0}^{k} (x^p-x)^ip^{k-i}A_i(x),\\]\n\n[b](b)[/b] Find a counter example for each prime $p$ and each $k > p$.",
    "username": "shoki",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_62562.png"
   }
